### What is a stack?

A stack is a linear data structure that follows Last In First Out ordering. The most recently added element sits on top, and it is the first element removed. You interact with a stack through three core operations: push to add, pop to remove and return the top, and peek to look at the top without removing it. All three operations run in constant time.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class ReceiptStack {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Deque<String> receipts = new ArrayDeque<>();
        receipts.push("TXN-1001: Rajesh deposited 50000");
        receipts.push("TXN-1002: Priya withdrew 20000");
        receipts.push("TXN-1003: Rajesh transferred 15000");

        System.out.println("Top receipt: " + receipts.peek());
        System.out.println("Popped: " + receipts.pop());
        System.out.println("New top: " + receipts.peek());
    }
}
```

```text
Top receipt: TXN-1003: Rajesh transferred 15000
Popped: TXN-1003: Rajesh transferred 15000
New top: TXN-1002: Priya withdrew 20000
```

### Why do we need a stack?

A stack exists because many real problems follow a last-in-first-out discipline. Undo features reverse actions in the opposite order they were performed. Balanced-bracket checkers close the most recently opened bracket first. Method calls return to the most recently entered method. All of these naturally fit LIFO ordering, which is exactly what a stack enforces.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class UndoService {
    private final Deque<String> actions = new ArrayDeque<>();

    public void perform(String action) {
        actions.push(action);
        System.out.println("Performed: " + action);
    }

    public String undo() {
        if (actions.isEmpty()) {
            return "Nothing to undo";
        }
        return "Undone: " + actions.pop();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        UndoService svc = new UndoService();
        svc.perform("Add payee HDFC-Priya");
        svc.perform("Set transfer limit 100000");
        svc.perform("Schedule transfer 50000");
        System.out.println(svc.undo());
        System.out.println(svc.undo());
    }
}
```

```text
Performed: Add payee HDFC-Priya
Performed: Set transfer limit 100000
Performed: Schedule transfer 50000
Undone: Schedule transfer 50000
Undone: Set transfer limit 100000
```

### How does a stack work internally?

ArrayDeque uses a resizable circular array under the hood. The head pointer tracks the top of the stack. When you push, the head moves one position and the element is placed there. When you pop, the element at the head is returned and the head moves back. Both operations are a single array access plus a pointer update, which is why they are constant time. The array doubles in size when full, giving amortized O(1) cost for push.

```java
package com.bank.core;

// Simplified stack internals to illustrate the concept
public class SimpleStack {
    private String[] data;
    private int top = -1;

    public SimpleStack(int capacity) {
        data = new String[capacity];
    }

    public void push(String item) {
        if (top == data.length - 1) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Stack overflow");
        }
        data[++top] = item;
    }

    public String pop() {
        if (top == -1) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Stack underflow");
        }
        String item = data[top];
        data[top--] = null; // help GC
        return item;
    }

    public String peek() {
        if (top == -1) {
            throw new RuntimeException("Stack is empty");
        }
        return data[top];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SimpleStack s = new SimpleStack(10);
        s.push("Account-1001");
        s.push("Account-1002");
        System.out.println("Top: " + s.peek());
        System.out.println("Popped: " + s.pop());
        System.out.println("Top after pop: " + s.peek());
    }
}
```

```text
Top: Account-1002
Popped: Account-1002
Top after pop: Account-1001
```

### How do you create and use a stack in Java?

In modern Java, use `ArrayDeque` through the `Deque` interface. The legacy `java.util.Stack` class extends `Vector` and is synchronized on every call, which adds unnecessary overhead. `ArrayDeque` also avoids exposing random-access methods that break the stack abstraction.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class BalanceTracker {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Recommended: ArrayDeque as a stack
        Deque<BigDecimal> balanceHistory = new ArrayDeque<>();

        balanceHistory.push(new BigDecimal("100000.00")); // opening balance
        balanceHistory.push(new BigDecimal("85000.00"));  // after withdrawal
        balanceHistory.push(new BigDecimal("125000.00")); // after deposit

        System.out.println("Current: " + balanceHistory.peek());
        System.out.println("Revert:  " + balanceHistory.pop());
        System.out.println("Previous: " + balanceHistory.peek());
        System.out.println("Size: " + balanceHistory.size());
    }
}
```

```text
Current: 125000.00
Revert:  125000.00
Previous: 85000.00
Size: 2
```

### How is a stack different from a queue?

A stack is LIFO and a queue is FIFO. The stack serves the newest element first, while the queue serves the oldest element first. Both are linear structures with O(1) core operations, but they model different real-world patterns.

| Feature | Stack (LIFO) | Queue (FIFO) |
|---|---|---|
| Order | Last In First Out | First In First Out |
| Add operation | push (top) | offer / enqueue (rear) |
| Remove operation | pop (top) | poll / dequeue (front) |
| Inspect operation | peek (top) | peek (front) |
| Java class | ArrayDeque (push/pop) | ArrayDeque (offer/poll) |
| Use case | Undo, DFS, expression eval | BFS, task scheduling, fairness |

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.Queue;

public class StackVsQueue {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Stack: LIFO
        Deque<String> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        stack.push("TXN-A");
        stack.push("TXN-B");
        stack.push("TXN-C");
        System.out.println("Stack pop: " + stack.pop()); // TXN-C (newest)

        // Queue: FIFO
        Queue<String> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
        queue.offer("TXN-A");
        queue.offer("TXN-B");
        queue.offer("TXN-C");
        System.out.println("Queue poll: " + queue.poll()); // TXN-A (oldest)
    }
}
```

```text
Stack pop: TXN-C
Queue poll: TXN-A
```

### What exceptions can occur?

Popping or peeking an empty `ArrayDeque` throws `NoSuchElementException`. The legacy `Stack` class throws `EmptyStackException`. Always guard with an `isEmpty()` check before calling pop or peek.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.NoSuchElementException;

public class StackExceptionDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Deque<String> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();

        // Wrong: pop without checking
        try {
            stack.pop();
        } catch (NoSuchElementException e) {
            System.out.println("Caught: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }

        // Correct: check before popping
        if (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println(stack.pop());
        } else {
            System.out.println("Stack is empty, nothing to pop");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Caught: NoSuchElementException
Stack is empty, nothing to pop
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is using the legacy `Stack` class instead of `ArrayDeque`. The `Stack` class extends `Vector`, which synchronizes every call and exposes `get(int)` which lets callers reach into the middle, breaking the LIFO contract.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.Stack;

// WRONG: using legacy Stack
public class LegacyStackMistake {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Stack<String> stack = new Stack<>();
        stack.push("Account-A");
        stack.push("Account-B");
        stack.push("Account-C");

        // This breaks the stack abstraction
        System.out.println("Middle access: " + stack.get(1));
        // Output: Account-B (violates LIFO)
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

// CORRECT: using ArrayDeque
public class ModernStackUsage {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Deque<String> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        stack.push("Account-A");
        stack.push("Account-B");
        stack.push("Account-C");

        // No get(int) method: LIFO contract preserved
        System.out.println("Top only: " + stack.peek());
    }
}
```

```text
Top only: Account-C
```

### When should you use a stack?

Use a stack whenever the problem has a last-in-first-out pattern. Classic examples include undo and redo, balanced parentheses checking, depth-first search, evaluating postfix expressions, and backtracking algorithms. If the most recently added item must be processed first, a stack is the right choice.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class DfsAccountHierarchy {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // DFS traversal of account hierarchy using a stack
        Deque<String> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        stack.push("National Bank HQ");

        while (!stack.isEmpty()) {
            String node = stack.pop();
            System.out.println("Visiting: " + node);
            if (node.equals("National Bank HQ")) {
                stack.push("Mumbai Branch");
                stack.push("Delhi Branch");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Visiting: National Bank HQ
Visiting: Delhi Branch
Visiting: Mumbai Branch
```

### What about thread safety?

ArrayDeque is not thread-safe. If multiple threads push and pop concurrently, the internal array can become corrupted. For concurrent use, wrap it with `Collections.synchronizedDeque()` or use a `ConcurrentLinkedDeque`. In banking transaction processing, each thread typically owns its own stack, so synchronization is rarely needed.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Deque;

public class ThreadSafeStackDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Not thread-safe
        Deque<String> unsafeStack = new ArrayDeque<>();

        // Thread-safe wrapper
        // Note: individual calls are safe, but compound check-then-act
        // still needs external synchronization
        // Deque<String> safeStack = Collections.synchronizedDeque(unsafeStack);

        // Best practice: each thread owns its own stack
        Deque<String> threadLocalStack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        threadLocalStack.push("TXN-5001");
        System.out.println("Thread-local pop: " + threadLocalStack.pop());
    }
}
```

```text
Thread-local pop: TXN-5001
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The JVM itself uses a stack for method calls. Each thread has a call stack where a frame is pushed on method entry and popped on return. This is why infinite recursion throws `StackOverflowError`: the call stack runs out of space. The default stack size is around 512 KB to 1 MB depending on the platform, and you can tune it with the `-Xss` flag.

ArrayDeque uses a power-of-two sized array and bitwise masking for fast index calculation. When the array fills, it doubles in size, so the amortized cost of push remains O(1). The old `Stack` class extends `Vector` which grows by 100% or a fixed increment and synchronizes every method, making it measurably slower in single-threaded code.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class CallStackDemo {
    static int depth = 0;

    public static void recurse() {
        depth++;
        recurse();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try {
            recurse();
        } catch (StackOverflowError e) {
            System.out.println("StackOverflowError at depth: " + depth);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
StackOverflowError at depth: 10234
```
